Subject: [PATCH] Use the VFS to mark WAL files as archived on shared storage

pgarch_archiveDone() strips the storage protocol from the archive-status
paths and passes them to the local rename() syscall. When pg_wal resides
on PFS, the .ready file belongs to shared storage, not the local
filesystem. The rename therefore fails with ENOENT even after the archive
command has successfully uploaded the WAL file.

Leaving .ready in place causes repeated archive attempts and prevents the
server from observing archive completion. In particular, pg_backup_stop()
with wait_for_archive enabled can keep waiting until a configured timeout
or cancellation, despite the required WAL already being in the archive.

Keep the protocol-qualified paths and call polar_rename() so the existing
VFS dispatches the operation to the appropriate storage implementation.
Use those same paths in the error message. Preserve the existing
non-durable rename semantics; no extra fsync is required by this change.

Validated on PolarDB 17 with shared PFS storage: automatic archiving
produced .done files, backup-stop completed with archive waiting enabled,
and WAL-G backup/PITR tests passed with both native Go and cgo clients,
including concurrent writes during backup.

---
--- a/src/backend/postmaster/pgarch.c
+++ b/src/backend/postmaster/pgarch.c
@@ -833,14 +833,8 @@
 	char		rlogready[MAXPGPATH];
 	char		rlogdone[MAXPGPATH];
 
-	const char *pure_rlogready;
-	const char *pure_rlogdone;
-
 	StatusFilePath(rlogready, xlog, ".ready");
 	StatusFilePath(rlogdone, xlog, ".done");
-
-	pure_rlogready = polar_path_remove_protocol(rlogready);
-	pure_rlogdone = polar_path_remove_protocol(rlogdone);
 
 	/*
 	 * To avoid extra overhead, we don't durably rename the .ready file to
@@ -849,11 +843,11 @@
 	 * function is called), so it should be okay if the .ready file reappears
 	 * after a crash.
 	 */
-	if (rename(pure_rlogready, pure_rlogdone) < 0)
+	if (polar_rename(rlogready, rlogdone) < 0)
 		ereport(WARNING,
 				(errcode_for_file_access(),
 				 errmsg("could not rename file \"%s\" to \"%s\": %m",
-						pure_rlogready, pure_rlogdone)));
+						rlogready, rlogdone)));
 }
 
 
